{"agent_id":"pp3g-yKAt-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T04:47:08.879843703Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"92a47d4f-4b7d-4dbc-975a-25991ca1746b","comment_uuid":"6a323972-dd32-4ada-8622-9062c0a9067b","author":"pp3g-yKAt-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","content":"# VERDICT — #135 unified agent lifecycle watcher plan\n\n**Bottom line: DIRECTIONALLY SOUND, STRUCTURALLY INCOMPLETE. Build it — but re-map the tiers, reuse what already exists, and harden detection before it is allowed to destroy anything.** The two-tier ordering (client-timeout → nudge → preserve+relaunch) is the right escalation shape, and Tier 1 (request-level timeout) is both the strongest and the cheapest part of the plan — it is also the ONLY tier that actually fixes the observed hang. The nudge tier already exists inside crosslink and is currently inert; the 2x-expected-interval rule was designed as an investigation trigger and is being promoted to a destruction trigger without the precision that requires; and the plan omits several existing mechanisms it should reuse plus the operational races it will create. Evidence below is from the live system (crosslink source at /home/claude-code/projects/crosslink, opencode 1.18.11, this repo's config, this session's running processes).\n\n---\n\n## (1) Nudge-first vs kill+relaunch — SOUND ORDERING, WRONG MECHANISM FOR THE OBSERVED FAILURE MODE\n\n**The ordering principle is right.** The hang is in the upstream request, not the agent's reasoning; the agent's context is intact; kill+relaunch destroys that context and should be last resort. Nudge-first is correct as an escalation *shape*.\n\n**But the nudge mechanism cannot unstick the failure mode it targets.** A tmux `send-keys` keystroke is buffered while the client is blocked waiting on the outgoing stream — the agent only reads it after the stream returns. It cannot make a hung stream return.\n\n**Evidence: the nudge already exists, is live, and the hangs happened anyway.** crosslink's kickoff ships a built-in watchdog that does exactly the proposed Tier 2 nudge: `launch.rs::build_watchdog_script` (launch.rs:84-121) sends `\"continue working, the task is not yet complete\"` via `tmux send-keys` after 300s heartbeat staleness, up to 5 nudges, 120s check interval (defaults in `types.rs:266-296` WatchdogConfig; spawned at launch.rs:667-672). It is default-enabled (absent config key → `WatchdogConfig::default()` with `enabled: true`), and it is running right now as a live sidecar for this session (`ps` shows the `NUDGES=0 sleep 300 ... send-keys` loop). The 3x observed hangs (laguna #129, YYRu #127, historical) all occurred with this watchdog armed — direct evidence that the nudge does not recover a hung stream.\n\n**Two further defects in the plan's nudge tier:**\n- **The built-in watchdog is currently INERT due to an exit-condition bug.** `launch.rs:88-89` — `if [ -f \"{worktree}/.kickoff-status\" ]; then exit 0; fi` — exits when the status file *exists*, but launch.rs writes the file (LAUNCHING/RUNNING) *before* spawning the watchdog (launch.rs:649-667), and nothing ever deletes it. So the watchdog exits at its first check and never nudges. The plan should FIX this condition (or check for a terminal sentinel) rather than build a second, parallel nudge that double-fires on the same tmux session.\n- **The proposed nudge message is doubly wrong.** \"your last request timed out — continue\" — if the request is still hung the agent never reads it; if Tier 1 worked, the agent already self-recovers and needs no nudge.\n\n**Conclusion:** nudge-first is sound as a *cheap probe before escalation* (it catches the different failure mode of idle-but-responsive agents — stuck loops, waiting on input) but it is not recovery for the hang. The plan should say so explicitly, and the effective first response to the observed failure mode is Tier 1 alone. This also means the watcher's destructive tier should be rare — good, that is what you want.\n\n---\n\n## (2) Tier 1 (request-timeout) — FEASIBLE VIA CONFIG, AND THE RIGHT PLACE\n\n**Feasible: yes, and at configuration level, not code level.** The installed `claude` binary is a wrapper that translates args to `opencode run` (verified: /home/claude-code/.local/bin/claude is a bash script exec'ing opencode). So every kickoff agent — regardless of the `agent.binary` setting (launch.rs:44-47, defaults \"claude\") — is an opencode process, and a user-level opencode config reaches all of them.\n\nopencode 1.18.11's provider config (verified against the live schema at opencode.ai/config.json) has exactly the needed knobs:\n- `provider.<id>.timeout` — full-request timeout in ms (`false` disables),\n- `provider.<id>.headerTimeout` — time to wait for response headers,\n- `provider.<id>.chunkTimeout` — \"Timeout in milliseconds between streamed SSE chunks... If no chunk arrives within this window, the request is aborted.\" **This is the direct fix for the \"outgoing stream never returns\" symptom**: a hung stream produces no SSE chunks, so chunkTimeout aborts and returns control to the agent loop.\n\n**The gap is real:** neither `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` nor `.opencode/opencode.json` defines any `provider` section — no timeouts are configured anywhere. The plan's assertion \"these requests apparently have no timeout configured\" checks out.\n\n**Caveats before declaring Tier 1 done:**\n1. **The `AI_RetryError` claim is UNVERIFIED in the installed binary.** What actually happens when `chunkTimeout` aborts mid-stream — a retryable error returned to the loop (agent continues) or a session-killing abort — must be tested empirically before building anything on it. This is the #1 open question of the whole design and the plan's sub-item (\"research whether opencode supports configuring per-request timeouts\") should be expanded into \"and verify the post-abort behavior\".\n2. **Value selection is the whole ballgame.** chunkTimeout must exceed the worst-case legitimate inter-chunk gap for reasoning-heavy models (deepseek-v4-*); set too tight, it manufactures the exact false-positive problem detection has (§3). Measure typical chunk cadence first.\n3. **Global user config also affects the operator's own interactive sessions** — probably desired, but state it explicitly.\n4. **CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR** set by kickoff (launch.rs:209-212) is claude-specific and ignored by the wrapper/opencode, so standard opencode config paths apply — good, but the implementation sketch should verify config reach in the launched environment.\n\n**Right place? Yes.** Fix at the point of failure: preserves context, zero runtime cost, binary-agnostic (all agents are opencode), and catches the hang in seconds-to-minutes rather than the watcher's interval-driven timescale. The watcher then handles only the residue — client crash, OS-level hang, retry loops, and agents that ignore the error. This is the correct division of labor.\n\n---\n\n## (3) Detection correctness — ADEQUATE FOR INVESTIGATION, NOT FOR DESTRUCTION\n\n**The 2x-expected-interval rule was designed as an investigation trigger, not an action trigger.** Playbook §5.3/#123: \"No checkpoint for >2x the expected interval = likely stalled... warrants investigation via `ps` and session status — do not wait for the timeout.\" Promoting it to an auto-kill/relaunch trigger raises the reliability bar substantially.\n\n**Signal inventory — the plan uses the wrong signals and misses the best one.** Mid-session, `.kickoff-status` mtime is *static* (the file is written at launch and only rewritten at completion), so \"file mtimes\" as proposed is nearly useless. The meaningful watcher-visible signals are: heartbeat file (`.crosslink/.cache/last-heartbeat`, written by heartbeat.py after every tool call, throttled 120s — the FINEST-grained liveness signal, already powering the crosslink watchdog), commit count (git log in the worktree), comment count (hub, after sync). **The plan never mentions the heartbeat file** — that is its biggest detection gap.\n\n**The fundamental limit the plan must accept:** for hosted models (deepseek-v4-*), \"provider is thinking\" and \"provider is hung\" are observationally IDENTICAL at the watcher level — in both cases the client sits idle on a socket. CPU-time won't discriminate (the client does not burn CPU while the provider reasons). Only the client can distinguish (chunks arriving vs not) — which is why Tier 1 is the precision instrument and the watcher is a blunt instrument. A status-file watcher cannot be made precise on this question.\n\n**False positives (STALLED on slow-but-working): REAL, at the destructive tier.** Long chain-of-thought (no tool calls → no heartbeat → no commits → no comments), verification phases (test/build/CI runs produce no agent-visible signals), an agent that posted all 4 checkpoints early then works long. For a 20m task: interval ≈ 5m (ceiling/4), 2x = 10m of silence → STALLED. A deepseek-v4-* model legitimately thinking through a hard step for 10-15m is indistinguishable. **False negatives (missed hang): LOW** — a hung stream stops all tool calls, so heartbeat + commits + comments freeze; heartbeat-based detection latency is ~120-300s + poll, far better than the plan's interval-based figure.\n\n**Hardening required before any action fires:**\n1. **Heartbeat staleness as the PRIMARY signal** (not status-file mtime), with commits+comments as corroboration.\n2. **Debounce:** require two consecutive detections before any action.\n3. **Per-model silence budget** derived from `.kickoff-metadata.json` `timeout_secs` (exists — verified; `is_timed_out()` in types.rs:380-398 already computes expiry from it) with a floor, plus a LAUNCHING grace period (setup/plan silence is normal — the built-in watchdog uses grace 300s for the same reason).\n4. **The DONE-missing completion edge (MUST FIX):** playbook §6.1 — \"DONE may be missing from agents that committed successfully but exited before writing the flag. The commit is ground truth.\" A finished-but-flagless agent looks exactly like a stall and would be killed+relaunched on an already-completed issue → duplicate work, branch conflicts. The watcher must classify \"branch has a final commit / issue has a result comment\" as COMPLETED regardless of `.kickoff-status`.\n\n**Verdict on (3):** catches true hangs: yes. False-positive-free on slow-but-working agents: NO at the destructive tier without the hardening above. With hardening + Tier 1 in place, acceptable; without them, the destructive tier will fire on healthy agents.\n\n---\n\n## (4) Risks\n\n- **Auto-relaunch masking systemic issues (THE risk).** The playbook's MANDATORY HALT (§7) and the §13 anti-pattern \"Retrying a failed delegation — masks systemic issues, wastes resources\" exist precisely to surface provider outages, model-catalog changes, rate limits, config drift. Auto-relaunching the same issue with the same model/provider re-hits the same wall, and the operator never sees the pattern. The escalation guard (max 2) bounds the blast radius but there is **no correlation logic**: if ≥2 agents stall within a window, that is a SYSTEMIC signal — the watcher should HALT immediately and NOT relaunch. There is also no cooldown/backoff between relaunches and no model/provider variation. Add all three.\n- **Token burn.** Bounded by max 2, but each relaunch re-reads the full issue. \"Resume from committed state\" must be implemented as a pointer to the preserved commit in the relaunch prompt, not a re-read (otherwise the retry-handoff from #115 is cosmetic). Include an explicit per-issue relaunch token budget in the policy doc. Note each nudge is itself a model call (token spend with no work).\n- **Worktree cleanup races.** (a) preserve-work `git add -A && git commit` races the agent's own git activity — index.lock conflicts, partial commits; the watcher must detect and skip/retry on a locked index. (b) Concurrent `crosslink kickoff cleanup` (playbook §4 pre-flight, §7.1 crash recovery) between kill and relaunch can remove the worktree/branch the relaunch depends on — the watcher must own the lifecycle or coordinate. (c) **Signing key (§8, §7.1):** if the worktree holds the driver key, deleting it orphans the key and breaks ALL hub commits — the relaunch path must re-assert the driver key (and preserve-work commits must use it).\n- **Sync/hydration interaction.** Comments posted by the watcher are invisible at the hub until `crosslink sync` (§5.4) — the \"record recovery on issue\" step must include sync. The watcher (a systemd timer, not an agent session) needs an identity to post comments at all. Relaunch requires `crosslink session work` on the issue, but the killed agent's session may still hold the lock — this repo already shows 7 stale locks, and blindly stealing risks clobbering a live session. The watcher must also exclude its own actions from liveness signals (self-referential activity).\n- **Watcher failure modes.** systemd restart double-fire → the state machine must be persisted and transitions idempotent; the plan mentions state but not restart semantics.\n\n---\n\n## (5) MISSING from the plan\n\n1. **REUSE/FIX the existing crosslink watchdog** — Tier 2's nudge already exists (launch.rs, default-enabled, live) and is currently inert due to the `-f` exit-condition bug (launch.rs:88-89). Configure/fix it instead of building a second nudge that fights over the same tmux session.\n2. **Heartbeat file as the primary liveness signal** (`.crosslink/.cache/last-heartbeat`) — 120s granularity vs. the plan's status-file mtime which is static mid-run.\n3. **The concrete Tier 1 change**: `provider.<id>.timeout/headerTimeout/chunkTimeout` in opencode.json (exact schema keys verified) + an experiment validating post-abort retry behavior (the AI_RetryError claim is unverified) + measured chunkTimeout values.\n4. **Commit-as-ground-truth completion edge** (§6.1) — finished-but-flagless agents must be COMPLETED, not STALLED.\n5. **Systemic-failure correlation** — ≥2 stalls in a window → HALT, no relaunch.\n6. **Relaunch parameters** — model/provider variation, cooldown/backoff, explicit token budget, and the #115 retry-handoff content in the relaunch prompt (pointer to preserved commit).\n7. **Operational races** — index.lock handling, coordination with `kickoff cleanup`, signing-key re-assertion, stale-lock handling for `session work`, watcher identity + mandatory sync-after-comment.\n8. **Expected-interval derivation** — from `.kickoff-metadata.json` `timeout_secs` (exists) with a floor and per-model adjustment; plus a LAUNCHING grace. The plan never pins down where \"expected interval\" comes from; without it the 2x rule is unfalsifiable.\n9. **CI_FAILED policy** — the state machine lists it but the actions (a)(b)(c) never handle it: explicit rule needed (suggest: notify, do NOT auto-relaunch — a code failure is not a hang).\n10. **Two-repo sync (§5.5)** — the policy-doc deliverable and any playbook change must be mirrored to tripn-astro in the same process; the plan is silent on this.\n11. **Prerequisites** — libnotify absent on this host (#133 verdict), webhook fallback for headless/remote, curl for webhook POST (#134 risk 6).\n12. **An experiment/validation step before build** — Tier 1 post-abort behavior, chunkTimeout values, and detection thresholds all need measurement before the policy doc is written.\n\n---\n\n## NOTABLE POSITIVES\n\n- Correct diagnosis of the failure mode (hung upstream stream, not broken agent reasoning) — which correctly points the fix at the client layer.\n- Tier 1 as the primary fix is the strongest architectural choice in the plan; it is cheap, binary-agnostic, and makes the destructive tier rare.\n- Escalation guard (max 2 relaunches then STOP) is the right HALT backstop, and the deliberate, operator-approved deviation from the never-auto-retry rule is the right thing to codify explicitly — just mirror it per §5.5 so the docs don't contradict each other.\n- Preserve-work-before-kill is the right instinct; it only needs the race hardening above.\n- Delivery mechanism (one systemd timer + stdlib Python watcher, zero Rust) matches the #133 verdict; #134's Path A remains the correct durable upgrade.\n- The plan already folds in #115 (retry handoff) and #123 (two-signal rule) — good reconciliation of prior research.\n\n**Recommendation:** proceed with the design, with this re-mapping: Tier 1 = opencode provider `chunkTimeout`/`timeout` config + retry-behavior experiment (primary recovery); Tier 2 = fix + configure the existing crosslink watchdog (nudge = cheap probe, not recovery); Tier 3 = watcher detect → preserve → kill → relaunch with correlation, cooldown, model variation, and the operational hardening above. The watcher's detection should be heartbeat-primary with debounce, per-model floors, and the commit-ground-truth completion edge, and no destructive action until Tier 1 is proven in production.","created_at":"2026-08-03T04:47:08.871032520Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:JRwxokEUeKPtJrC2z3PyixJ6AJU5S6Hvc9jslQp4W0Q","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgJfwSG2am+Fg3k1Aj3sFSOt1nVE4gWVKSZS2lcafpOa8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAir7Pr/DQa68b58+rYbtL93b5h4eY9rC3/dqFEXRBB37Sxnik9MbRrHufPVC4/tmg64hEWE0pqr3ikFNn8rCpAg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:JRwxokEUeKPtJrC2z3PyixJ6AJU5S6Hvc9jslQp4W0Q","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgJfwSG2am+Fg3k1Aj3sFSOt1nVE4gWVKSZS2lcafpOa8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGnSLfoPtboyTOxFjcD8UaTxfwiK+3tuzaGFGjdcYrr8+LiE4ojdNwHu4tY9Q2jDxuZoZ2n/9a8be82EMVcLUgM="}
